WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.250
So we're living in the early days

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.965
of a massive paradigm shift.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.690
We are accelerating into a digitized

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.240
and decentralized future.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.400
That is why Bitcoin is here.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.730
The cryptocurrency market

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.720
has roughly a $3 trillion market cap.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.160
That's the huge milestone.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.420
This is the largest fastest
wealth creation event

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.760
in the history of the world

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.520
and financial institutions

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.830
have only now just figured this out.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.420
The first futures ETF tied to Bitcoin.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.710
This thing is becoming
increasingly entangled

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.440
with the mainstream financial markets.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.273
The world's going there.

00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:36.460
You know, Bank of America

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.320
is now fully engaging in
analysis of crypto broadly.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.040
Never at any point

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.220
have we had the freedom
of financial mobility

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.030
that we do have now with
blockchain and DeFi.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.040
It is a power to the
people kind of technology.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.750
We're moving to a new kind of world now

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.410
where users own their own data,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.120
and they get to run their
own financial services.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.760
However, we are still tied

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.730
to the centralized banking system

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.233
in a way that is unnecessary.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.810
The crypto sector today
is largely unregulated.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.400
This is a little bit of
like the American wild west.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.690
Essentially, what DeFi is,
it's like a big casino.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.113
That's scary to the JPMorgans

00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:15.600
because they're seeing all this
growth and they're left out.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.430
You all can do whatever
you want and I don't care.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.740
You're either in or out.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.990
There's no in-between.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.080
It's pretty much full
service shadow banking

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.290
with nobody in charge.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.200
There's an old adage in technology

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.810
where they say software is
going to eat everything,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.700
and one can say very clearly

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.470
that blockchain is
going to eat everything.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.810
We're seeing a revolution
unfold right now.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.390
These are historic times.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.370
Buckle your seatbelt.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.615
This is where the ride
gets really interesting.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.493
My jacket's in 414 or something.

00:01:51.758 --> 00:01:53.430
Oh, yeah.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.240
We got the presidential suite downstairs

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.810
for the after party.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.618
I just, okay.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.450
And I left my jacket there

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.993
and I just, I will check it on my way out.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.087
Coffee, black.

00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:11.130
Bitcoin was the beginning, right?

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.940
Bitcoin is where it all started.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.130
What are cryptocurrencies?

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.450
They're a type of digital money,

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.610
but instead of being controlled
by a central government

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.550
they're decentralized using
blockchain technology.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.870
The impetus was the financial
crash of 2008, right?

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.710
That's in the white paper of bitcoin.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:30.610
2008 and Bitcoin gave us

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.240
the first spark that
we needed in order to,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.130
you know, start the fire.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.480
We had the same basically
financial systems

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.250
in place for hundreds of years.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.040
And now we have this new type
of ledger that's actually

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.920
self-auditing by the virtue
of being decentralized.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.980
So that is really the
core of this technology.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.430
So what was once very
obscure magic internet money

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.760
is now a global financial infrastructure

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.790
that anybody can build on
and anybody can access.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:58.960
And what this whole movement has grown

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.480
into has been like an
evolution of the technology.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.520
The vision has really
always been the same,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.620
decentralizing, democratizing,
returning power to users

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.740
of finance or really any
web-based application.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.060
So we're effectively
building an entire, you know,

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.800
new world ourselves, right?

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.020
This, you know, this, $2 trillion industry

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.360
has bubbled up in 12 years.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.760
Soon a majority of the
world's billionaires

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.500
will have made their
money from cryptocurrency.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.880
I don't mean many, a majority.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:28.790
This is super new.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.720
If you think about Bitcoin
again, the most mainstream

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.020
digital asset in the
cryptocurrency universe.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.600
That was only released in 2008,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.550
and it really didn't come into mainstream

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.623
consciousness until 2017.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.120
One of the best ways to view
the maturation of Bitcoin is,

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.414
if you look at mining.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:56.460
This used to be something

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.300
that you could mine in your dorm room.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.670
I know a few people who did that.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.070
To now, you basically have
to have a very sophisticated

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:06.620
operation to really mine Bitcoin at scale,

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.210
to solve these increasingly
complex computer problems.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.923
That takes a lot of electricity.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:18.140
Bit Digital is a Bitcoin miner,

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.200
and we've been mining
Bitcoin in various states

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.800
like Nebraska, New York,
Georgia, and Texas and so on.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.530
Bit Digital in particular,
looks for clean sources

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.060
of power in order to power our computers

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.250
that help solve these
mathematical problems

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.580
that produce Bitcoin.

00:04:35.373 --> 00:04:36.888
Can't hear a damn thing in here.

00:04:36.888 --> 00:04:38.638
Correct, it's pretty loud.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.320
This is our warehouse
in Kearney, Nebraska.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:51.140
Over here we've got a
bunch of unboxed units.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:53.010
There's about 2,000 here.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.960
And you know, frankly,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.140
it's not great to see
them sitting here in boxes

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.390
because they could be
plugged in and making money.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:00.850
What are these, M21s?

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.930
Yeah, these are all M20s.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.830
M20s, depending on the model

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.140
and the exact moment you buy them

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.090
are gonna cost 10 to 15,000 a piece.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.100
They're a lot heavier than they look

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.130
and you plug it in and it
starts making this commodity

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.560
we know as Bitcoin.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:19.750
Bitcoin is the mother coin.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.490
It's the first coin, it's
the first blockchain.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:23.760
But I think beyond that,

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:27.370
Bitcoin has had a bit of
a democratizing effect.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:30.880
It's created opportunities
to generate capital.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.090
We wouldn't have had
access to that before.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.730
You know, that was enabled by Bitcoin.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.430
And now it's spread to a bigger ecosystem.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.850
The basic business model of Bitcoin mining

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:44.550
has traditionally been to
accumulate as much Bitcoin

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.720
as possible and hold that Bitcoin

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.060
for its appreciation potential.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.303
But there's a broader vision here.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:59.780
Lending, borrowing, stock
exchanges, currency exchanges,

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.970
insurance, derivatives,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:05.210
all of these now have their facsimiles

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:06.440
in the digital realm,

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.850
which had been created with code

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.830
rather than with brick
and mortar institutions.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.060
And this is the landscape

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.853
that we are thinking of
when we say the word DeFi.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.930
Trying to study and
wrap my head around DeFi

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.050
a lot more lately.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:22.730
Four or five years ago, you know,

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:24.730
everything we were doing
was really oriented around

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:26.710
Bitcoin and its growing ecosystem.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.130
Over the past few years in contrast,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.220
everything has been oriented around DeFi.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.250
DeFi is a collection of software projects

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:36.060
that run on the blockchain that enable

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.570
a host of money activities.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:39.820
Things like lending and borrowing,

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.520
swapping or trading of different assets.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.653
The creation of new
asset classes like NFTs.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.365
You go and deposit money in
a bank and, what, you get

00:06:50.365 --> 00:06:53.530
.75% interest at best.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:55.560
Depositing in a bank,
but then they loan it out

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.860
at 10% to 20% to people.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.940
So they're making 20 times the money

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.690
that they're giving you in return.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.440
Whereas like DeFi can
definitely change that paradigm.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.760
There are two promises to DeFi.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:08.540
And I think they broadly fork.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:10.760
So you have the idea
of financial inclusion,

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.470
where people who do not have
access to financial services

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.050
can get that access.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:16.570
And then you have the idea

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.360
that for the rest of the world

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.710
that already had access
to financial services,

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:23.580
were now switching from
snail mail to email.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:25.410
and I think it's exciting and unknown

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.670
what emergent properties
will come out of that,

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.100
when you can transact much faster

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.870
and in a much more trustless way.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.530
I don't need five people to guarantee

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:35.780
that a transaction is gonna happen

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.020
if I can just automate it through a code.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:41.260
Because the code is
available for anybody to see,

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.100
and that code is immutable,

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.880
you should be able to
simply look at that and say,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:45.930
yes this code is doing

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:47.750
what that person is
saying they're gonna do,

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.830
I can verify, not trust.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.820
The decentralized finance system is,

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:54.790
we believe a disruptive technology,

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.350
not unlike the early days of the internet,

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:00.660
that is eventually going to replace

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.590
large swaths of the
financial and monetary system

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:04.739
as we know it.

00:08:04.739 --> 00:08:07.630
The potential for it to
really disrupt finance

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:09.140
and the banking system,

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.710
a lot of that is still very much a dream,

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:13.950
a vision of the crypto community,

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.920
but its potential applications
are enormous for sure.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.930
If you think about the overall
cryptocurrency universe,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.040
that's estimated to be $3 trillion.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.790
Estimates for how much of that is in DeFi

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:29.920
is in the hundreds of
billions, 250 billion.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.280
The speed of innovation is incredible.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.620
There's compounding
exponential innovation.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.990
And it's driven by the
openness of the community,

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.170
both from a software perspective,

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:43.750
but also a really genuine desire

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.052
to continue to add to innovations.

00:08:49.919 --> 00:08:54.720
The blockchain is the underpinning
of all of this change.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.370
So blockchain is the
record keeping technology.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.890
It's this decentralized public ledger

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.060
where all of these
cryptocurrency transactions

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.960
are recorded anonymously.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.860
So the cryptocurrency is
the medium of exchange

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.240
and the blockchain is
where that's all recorded.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:13.160
Blockchain is good at three things.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:15.920
It's good at storing information,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.880
security, and allowing anyone to audit it.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.180
That is the ultimate weapon, dare I say,

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.500
to fight back against people

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.870
who have been hiding information,

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:27.930
who have been threatening security,

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:30.540
who have been manipulating information.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.300
It's a decentralized system,

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:34.940
no one owns or controls or runs it.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.340
Meaning, if you wanted to stop this thing,

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.690
that's facilitating this
change, how do you do it?

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.010
You know, where's the leader?

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.070
Where's the CEO?

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:42.970
Where's the company?

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.280
There isn't one.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.880
You can't shut down that system,

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:49.890
and it's the most secure
system the world has ever seen.

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.303
And so that is what
makes this all possible.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.280
The next evolution for a lot of people

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.703
is then the Ethereum network.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.520
Ethereum has expanded the possibilities

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.160
of what can be done with this technology.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.000
We understood that the technology worked.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.723
From there it was kind of
like a very quick click.

00:10:16.911 --> 00:10:18.680
Hi, my name is Joseph Lubin.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:23.210
Yeah so I was one of eight original

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:24.970
co-founders of Ethereum.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.530
The Ethereum project set out to become

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:30.570
a new trust foundation for the planet,

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.610
to essentially effect a paradigm shift

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.270
in how the world organizes itself.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.140
In order to be a new trust foundation,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:42.140
you need to be massively
decentralized in as many dimensions

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:44.580
of decentralization as you can identify.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:46.930
And the new trust foundation
gives us things like

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:50.570
digital scarcity, it gives
us trustworthy collaboration.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.650
It enables us to transact

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.550
between people who don't know one another,

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:58.190
just because you can trust the programs

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:01.210
that are guaranteed to
execute on the blockchain.

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:04.570
It seemed very natural to call
this emerging decentralized

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:06.600
protocol, blockchain, cryptocurrency,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.393
set of technologies, Web3.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:15.967
What is Web3?

00:11:15.967 --> 00:11:17.700
Can you explain it simply?

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.674
Let's start with Web1, how about that?

00:11:22.953 --> 00:11:26.380
Web1, for the first time we
had access to information.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:27.460
Welcome.
We had this kind

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.120
of real idealistic

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:33.690
notion of knowledge and
communication and information.

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:34.650
Goodbye.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:37.110
Web2 happened, we saw a big consolidation.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.400
You know, Facebook,
Twitter, so on and so forth.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:44.110
And Web3 is really
taking the ideals of Web1

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.800
and kind of solidifying
them in a meaningful way.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:48.970
Web3 is really shorthand

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.620
for a world where the users
are creating the value,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.720
but also capturing the value.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:55.030
Something that was totally lost

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.540
with the Facebooks and the Googles

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:57.840
and the Amazons of the world.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:59.760
The people, the users are the ones

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.330
that have much more power,
that are able to create

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:05.380
much more value and also
are able to consequently

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:06.770
capture some of that value.

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:09.230
It's known as this amalgamation

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:11.500
of these different
decentralized technologies.

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.290
And this new world, essentially
that we're creating.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:14.910
The first wave of the internet, right,

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.190
took multiple years.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.940
Web2 unfolded over the course of,

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:20.190
you know, 10, 12 years.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.850
And so Web3, it's just beginning.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:25.600
And so that's the phase
that we're in right now.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:28.093
Early on, we built smart contracts.

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:32.100
Probably the most interesting part

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.540
of the Ethereum network is
that it uses smart contracts.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:38.770
Which are basically these
self-executing contracts

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:41.675
based on preset conditions.

00:12:41.675 --> 00:12:43.500
And when those conditions take place,

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:47.980
there is an exchange of value
that happens automatically.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:49.523
If then, this, that.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:12:58.750
If you think about the floor

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:01.080
of the New York stock exchange,

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.410
or if you think about simply
buying or selling stock

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:05.640
in your brokerage account,

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.580
there are fees paid that are
associated with doing that.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.780
Contrast that with clicking
a button on your computer,

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:14.390
without any human beings touching it,

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:17.060
and without any transaction fees.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.710
That becomes much more efficient

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.923
from an economic perspective for the user.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.850
What we've done is we've identified

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.780
certain DeFi protocols.

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:28.980
Then you could generate
a very good return.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.700
It's all governed by code.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.370
It's just software running in the cloud.

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:35.860
There aren't armies of human beings

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:37.890
working in office towers,

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.970
earning bonuses to facilitate this.

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:43.710
We're not participating
in DeFi at this time,

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:46.110
just because there's too
much regulatory uncertainty

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.800
for a public company.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.470
So what we're doing
right now in the interim

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:50.520
is we're studying it.

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:57.360
My name is Tim Massad.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:58.790
I was formerly the chairman

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.120
of the Commodity Futures
Trading Commission.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:04.760
Well, the crypto sector
today is largely unregulated.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:07.970
It's hard to know whether
a particular DeFi app

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:09.810
is going to work as promised

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:12.640
or whether it was created
by reputable people,

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:15.240
unless you've done a lot of due diligence.

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:18.820
How can a typical person
even begin to, say,

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:23.820
identify, like, what a good
project is, in this space?

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:25.359
I mean there's no question

00:14:25.359 --> 00:14:28.160
that the token economy is a very volatile

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:30.050
asset class to be involved in.

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:32.520
There's tens of thousands
of cryptocurrencies

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.640
and NFTs being created every day.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:37.560
And some of these things are
gonna go off a cliff to zero.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:39.700
And so buyer beware.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.670
The obvious risk is that, you know,

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.730
there is no counterparty to fall back on.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:46.530
If you do lose your money
somehow, which theoretically,

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.010
you're not supposed to be
able to with a smart contract,

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.230
there's no recourse here.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.130
You have, you don't have a
counterparty to turn back to,

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:54.830
that's why prime brokers exist.

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.750
That's why they calculate risk rules.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:15:00.030
That's why exchanges
facilitate these transactions.

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:03.383
But by removing them, you
also remove that buffer.

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:06.870
Right now, if I mistype
the address in Ethereum,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.740
that money could be gone forever, right?

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.520
And my aunt Matilda can't
lose her life savings

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:12.570
using Ethereum that way.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.050
It's just not usable or
sustainable for somebody like her.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:16.870
So in order to go mainstream,

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:20.930
you may need to invite
some centralized aspects.

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:23.950
Most of us can't spend hours figuring out

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:27.720
if a particular DeFi app is
going to work as promised

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.380
or studying the code.

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:32.500
So we rely on others for expertise,

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:34.970
for certain services, and intermediaries

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:37.050
often perform those functions.

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.250
The real value of a regulatory framework

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.170
is that it gives people confidence.

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:45.460
It gives them confidence that
whatever financial activity

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:49.480
they're participating in or
intermediary they're relying on

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:52.230
is going to do what it promised.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.140
That they're not going to be the victim

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:55.860
of fraud and manipulation,

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:59.363
that no one's going to
abscond with their money.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.680
This is a little bit like the
American Wild West, right?

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:09.020
Like there's this cutting
edge frontier out there,

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:10.510
freelance, stake your claim

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:12.340
on the potential gold rush, right?

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.290
But when you get out there, it's,

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:15.847
hey, there's people trying to rob you

00:16:15.847 --> 00:16:18.040
and there's scams going on and...

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.930
Blockchain and cryptocurrencies

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.860
are a disruptive technology.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:27.140
They're a young technology,
there will be exploits.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:28.490
Federal investigators say

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.520
a Wild West culture has
led to a massive spike

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:33.400
in cryptocurrency scams.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:35.820
There are a whole list
of problems with crypto.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.580
Unreliable tech, scams.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.780
You have like these Ponzi scheme projects

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:44.970
where their tokens are priced
- basically crap coins, right?

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:47.260
Or you have these other
projects where you deposit

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:49.100
your liquidity and then they do a rug pull

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:52.050
where they basically steal
the money out from under you.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.450
Or basically these just
fly by night projects,

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:56.920
where they never really had
the intention to build it,

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:58.420
but they just kind of strung you along

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.860
and then they close up shop eventually.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:03.770
Regulators need to step
up to address crypto's

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:07.240
regulatory gaps and ensure
that we're actually building

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:10.073
the inclusive financial
system that we need.

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:14.070
Essentially what DeFi is,

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:15.890
it's like a big casino.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:17.560
It provides people with an opportunity

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:19.990
to make potentially huge gains

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:22.520
or potentially lose a ton of money.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:24.790
But for the people who
end up being the house,

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:29.230
running the casino, it's
pretty much guaranteed gain.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.400
The intermediate, they're the exchanges,

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:33.400
they're the DeFi platforms.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:36.110
And beyond that, they don't
have any of the regulations

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:39.490
that guarantee safety,
soundness and protect customers.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:42.643
It may ultimately produce some
really useful innovations.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:46.180
But for the time being, it's
pretty much full service

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:48.453
shadow banking with nobody in charge.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:51.210
Throughout the financial system

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.340
we have what are known as Know
Your Customer requirements,

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:58.740
which means that banks, brokers,
and other financial actors

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:02.060
have to verify the
identity of their customer.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.710
And that is a means to
prevent illicit activity.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:07.730
It also helps reduce fraud.

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:12.180
The DeFi sector will have to
figure out how to do this.

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:15.810
And I think really the only
way the sector can grow

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:19.840
and become mainstream is if
you have a regulatory framework

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:23.040
that sets some baseline standards.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.060
We welcome regulation, we're eager for it

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:26.180
because we need clarity

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:27.940
and there's so much
opportunity in this space

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:29.380
we wanna participate in.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:30.970
What I'd say is that we're hopeful

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:32.840
that the regulation's crafted in a way

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:36.780
that protects individual
investors and spurs innovation.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:37.740
And doesn't just protect

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.313
the interests of incumbent
financial institutions.

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:42.830
So what are these guys doing?

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:44.460
Why are they going to these great lengths?

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.810
Are they trying to bring about,

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:51.480
a more stable, equitable,
inclusive kind of finance?

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:53.800
Or are they looking to get rich?

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.593
I would tend to believe the latter.

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:10.080
Miami has a very crypto friendly vibe.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.310
And I think the industry is taking off.

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:15.460
It's hard to imagine what
decentralization looks like.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.340
You come here though
and you see real people

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:20.300
who you've met online, or, hey,
I threw this metaverse party

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:22.550
and you were there and I'm
meeting you in real life.

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:26.190
It's all these people who
love all these esoteric pieces

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:27.890
of the cryptocurrency universe,

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:29.870
that now come together and
they can share the things

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:30.870
that they're passionate about.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:32.700
They can share their enthusiasm,

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.358
they can share their expertise.

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:38.740
Their minds are open
to ideas, to dreaming,

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:40.950
to thinking big, to thinking differently.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:42.564
As we're re-imagining money,

00:19:42.564 --> 00:19:45.950
we're really creating
this equality future.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:49.240
I think we're gonna slowly
change this disparity

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:51.260
between the rich and the poor.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:54.750
This whole event here is
still very, very early

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:56.330
compared to what's gonna happen,

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:58.740
or what's about to happen
in crypto and NFTs.

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:00.700
And this space in general.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:03.110
DeFi is actually in
the heart, in the skin,

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:05.100
everything of what we do.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:06.480
It's exciting to see how many people

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:07.610
are getting into this space.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:11.180
Art has sort of joined forces with tech.

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:14.750
There's enough space for everyone in here.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:22.590
So we're living in the early days

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:24.500
of a massive paradigm shift.

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:27.030
We are accelerating,

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:30.830
into a digitized and decentralized future

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:33.800
where there will be a
better trust foundation.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:37.690
So similar to how web technology
and internet technology

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:40.990
brought democracy and democratization

00:20:40.990 --> 00:20:42.510
to access to information,

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:45.880
we're bringing technologists
and entrepreneurs in,

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.023
to democratize global finance.

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:52.990
So I actually just left my job

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.980
for 10 years at Goldman Sachs

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:56.760
as a VP in the risk division.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:20:58.990
You know, covering hedge
funds and prime services.

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:01.760
My last day I caught a flight
to come to this convention

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:03.670
and really start participating

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:05.763
in the decentralized finance space.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:09.030
As I started to realize,

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:12.450
I don't know, the lack
of necessity of my own

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:13.740
role that I was building.

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:17.240
My legacy helps a system

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:20.280
that I don't believe is
fully necessary anymore

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:23.560
and a system that doesn't benefit

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:27.160
the broad public or communities
that really need it.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:28.380
It's hard to read,

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:31.110
but if you look at the
placard for the convention,

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.520
the Wifi password is kind of self-evident

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:36.480
about how a lot of these people here feel.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.150
You know, there's an
entire financial ecosystem

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:39.140
that's being modeled

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:41.520
and created and championed

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:43.730
by, you know, a bunch of apes

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:46.140
or you know other kind of like avatars.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:48.170
And that's scary to the JPMorgans

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:49.910
because again, they're
seeing all this growth

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:50.860
and they're left out.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:53.230
Jamie Dimon has a very specific opinion

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:55.520
on cryptocurrency, he
really thinks it's a scam.

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:57.500
I could care less what Bitcoin trades for,

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:00.010
how it trades, why it
trades, who trades it,

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:01.080
if you're stupid enough to buy,

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:02.930
you'll pay the price for it one day.

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:04.620
But that doesn't stop JPMorgan

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:06.270
from having an entire department

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:08.320
devoted to blockchain technology.

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:10.360
There are so many people involved

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.290
in the traditional banking system.

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:14.560
I don't think the goal is to remove them,

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:17.430
but the question is, what is necessary?

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:20.670
Should we be doing things the
way we're doing them today,

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:22.950
simply because we've always done that?

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:24.720
We don't do that with telephones.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:25.940
We don't do that with cars.

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:28.030
We don't do that with
all the other technology.

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:32.310
However, we are still tied to
the centralized banking system

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:34.020
in a way that is unnecessary.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:35.930
So major financial institutions

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:39.300
are for the large part
aware of the technology.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:43.557
Many of them have strict regulation,

00:22:43.557 --> 00:22:47.170
that they have to pay attention
to and are constrained by.

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:50.550
Maybe some of them will
put their head in the sand

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.640
and think all of this will go away.

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:55.440
They won't last very long, I assume.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:56.420
It's either, you know,

00:22:56.420 --> 00:22:59.760
come in and participate
or basically die, right?

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:02.020
Or get displaced in some form or fashion.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.760
So I know that banks are working

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:06.025
on their own blockchain solutions.

00:23:06.025 --> 00:23:08.400
They're keeping up with
everything that's going on.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:10.380
And it's the same with the governments.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.380
They're not really
talking about this stuff,

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:13.213
but they're there,

00:23:13.213 --> 00:23:14.270
they're listening and they're learning

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:16.223
because they know that this is the future.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:20.710
As you think about, you know,

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:22.660
the billions of people that
are coming onto the internet

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:24.440
first time, having an internet

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:26.120
native financial infrastructure

00:23:26.120 --> 00:23:27.760
is gonna allow all of these people

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:30.000
that are normally
unbanked to become banked.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.450
And it's definitely gonna be very big

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:34.043
for financial inclusivity
across the entire world.

00:23:34.963 --> 00:23:37.970
We're not waiting for the
big banks to open the door,

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:39.480
we're not waiting for the federal reserve

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:40.920
to give a thumbs up.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:44.100
People are able to create and cultivate

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.770
their own communities, their
own ideas, their own products,

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.150
and capture more of that value directly

00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:50.490
with fewer middlemen.

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.580
That's really the promise of blockchain.

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:55.540
And so we're at that stage of
this industry's development

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:58.800
where everybody's starting to get it.

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:01.610
You know, consensus just being reached

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:04.080
amongst all the industries
that this is real

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:05.480
and it ain't going anywhere.

